  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1235 AM MDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nActive weather looks to continue into early next week before\ndifferences arise in the deterministic and ensemble solutions.\nThe main differences beyond Monday deal with positioning of\nstrength of the rebuilding upper high across the western states.\n\nFriday through Sunday...models in good agreement through this\nperiod with the upper high over the Southeast states and broad\nsouthwesterly flow continuing across Colorado. Several embedded\nupper waves are forecast to track northeast across Colorado\nthrough the weekend, each bringing the potential for showers and\nthunderstorms.   \n\nThe first embedded wave will lift across Colorado on Friday.\nPWAT values remain elevated over an inch across the Plains and\nlow level flow continues to keep moisture pooled along the\nEastern Mountains. The NAM is most aggressive with the embedded\nupper wave, sparking convection across the Eastern Mountains by\nearly afternoon. Modest CAPE and shear and the high PWAT values\nwill once again enable storms to be heavy rain producers. The \nNAM lights up the whole of the Eastern Mountains, with higher- \nres guidance is a mix of focus areas, mainly the Palmer Divide \ndepending on the upper vort max track. Given the uncertainty, \ndid not issue any Flash Flood Watch for Friday, but know that \none may be needed as models converge on a solution. Temperatures\ncontinue to cool on Friday, with mostly 80s across the lower \nelevations.\n\nNot much change on Saturday, with another wave moving across\nColorado. This will once again bring more showers and\nthunderstorms to the region. Guidance remain focused on the\nPalmer Divide and for areas north of Highway 50. Heavy rainfall\nwill be the main concern and the flash flood risk remains\nelevated on areas burn scars. Temperatures continue to be\npleasant, with 80s across the Plains.\n\nBy Sunday, the upper high to the southeast begins to retrograde\nwest into Texas. This will bring one more day of southwesterly\nflow aloft with embedded energy. PWAT values begin to fall, and\nrainfall efficiencies decrease. Still, any thunderstorms that\nmove across burn scars will be capable of producing localized\nflash flooding. Temperatures on Sunday will continue to be nice,\nwith 70s and 80s for highs.\n\nMonday into Thursday...large differences in the models arrive\nnext week. The one consistency between guidance and ensembles \nis a drying and warming trend, with the main differences coming\nwith the position and strength of the retrograding upper ridge.\nThe ECMWF is strongest with the upper high across Colorado for \nmid to late week. It is the warmest and driest solution. The GFS\nis a bit further west with the high over the Great Basin. This \nsolution brings energy in northwest flow across the state a mid \nweek cold front. Overall, NBM temperature guidance has a slow \nwarming trend throughout next week, with mid 90s on the Plains \nby Wednesday and Thursday. The NBM guidance is also on the drier\nside, with showers and thunderstorms mainly confined to the \nhigher terrain.",
